# AGENTS.md — Solon SFS adapter

Use Solon through the global `sfs` runtime. In Codex prefer `$sfs ...` or
`sfs ...`; treat `/sfs ...` as Solon only if the text reaches the model.

Read order:
1. `SFS.md`
2. `sfs context path kernel`
3. `sfs context path index`
4. Only the routed context module for the current command via `sfs context path ...`.

Rules:
- Run `sfs <command>` first; adapter stdout/stderr is SSoT and stays verbatim.
- `sfs profile` may refine only `SFS.md` `## 프로젝트 개요`.
- Bash-first means no artifact refinement; it does not forbid a compact Next.
- AI-era fundamentals apply across all gates, not only implement: shared design
  concept, domain language, feedback loop, interface/artifact boundary, and
  gray-box delegation.
- For implementation and review work, follow the routed context guardrails:
  surface material assumptions, choose the smallest useful slice, keep changes
  surgical, read actual files/errors before fixing, verify before completion,
  and report exact evidence.
- `sfs brainstorm` has depth. `--simple` is quick requirement cleanup, default normal asks a few
  owner-thinking questions, and `--hard` keeps plan blocked while important
  owner decisions are unresolved.
- Start from `sfs status`; use current sprint `report.md` only when one exists.
- Use `docs/solon/` for shared adoption/handoff summaries. `.sfs-local/` is
  private local workbench state and should stay gitignored unless the team opts in.
- Stop on mutex conflicts and report owner/domain.
- Treat `sfs review` as artifact acceptance review. Code review is only the
  `code` lens; otherwise follow the adapter's auto-selected lens.
- In Solon reports, show gates as `Gate N (Name)`, not naked ids:
  Gate 1 (Intake), Gate 2 (Brainstorm), Gate 3 (Plan),
  Gate 4 (Design), Gate 5 (Handoff), Gate 6 (Review),
  Gate 7 (Retro). Use gate numbers 1..7 for new CLI examples.
- Solon reports should feel like compact console dashboards, not flat bullet
  dumps: title/verdict strip, 2-4 labeled status panels, one action rail, and
  at most 1-3 questions.
- Decision questions must be self-contained: before any `Q1`, `D1`, or option
  id, explain in plain user language what is being decided, why it matters, the
  recommended default, and what each option changes. Labels are
  cross-references, not the explanation.
